    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests passing, no failures or errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented `capacityWarning` as a persisted boolean flag on an owner, set at creation time to `true` when the owner's city already holds 40\u201349 owners (approaching the hard limit of 50). The hard rejection at 50 is unchanged.\n\nI followed the codebase's existing warning-flag pattern (`bulkSignupWarning`) and, rather than copying the city-counting logic, extracted it into a shared helper so both the limit check and the warning use one source of truth:\n\n- **`OwnerCities.java`** (new) \u2014 shared `countIn(repository, city)` helper mirroring the existing `OwnerRegistrations` helper.\n- **`RequireCityCapacity.java`** \u2014 refactored to use `OwnerCities.countIn(...)` (removed the duplicated inline stream); the `CAPACITY = 50` rejection is untouched.\n- **`FlagOwnerCityCapacityWarning.java`** (new) \u2014 single-responsibility step that sets `capacityWarning = count >= 40 && count < 50`, reusing `RequireCityCapacity.CAPACITY` as the shared limit constant.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `flagCityCapacityWarning` step (with `govern: [ transaction ]`) right after `build`, so it runs on the built `Owner` before `save`.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` column field with getter/setter, beside `bulkSignupWarning`.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the OwnerDto schema (MapStruct auto-maps it by name, so no mapper change needed).\n- **db schemas** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column.\n\nBecause the count is over *existing* stored owners (the new owner is saved later), filling a city to 40 and creating the 41st sees `count == 40`, yielding `capacityWarning = true` \u2014 matching the acceptance test.",
